# Template for the Compose example. Copy it to `.env`, which is git-ignored:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# There is no default token, and Compose will not start without one. Generate a
# value and paste it in:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# An environment variable keeps the token out of the command line, so it does not
# appear in `ps`. That is all it buys. Docker records the value in the container's
# configuration, where `docker inspect` and `docker compose config` render it for
# anyone who can reach the daemon; inside the container it is readable through
# `/proc/<pid>/environ`; and here it sits in plain text in `.env`, which git
# ignores but does not encrypt. Restrict access to the daemon, this Compose file,
# and this env file, and do not mistake one shared token for secret-store
# isolation - a real secret store is a separate decision this example does not
# make for you. Keep the value out of the repository and out of any image layer.

TEXTFLOWKIT_API_TOKEN=
